WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.322 --> 00:00:03.144
(whooshing)
(bomb blast)

00:00:03.144 --> 00:00:04.986
- Hello, Believe Nation,
today, we're going to talk

00:00:04.986 --> 00:00:08.983
about the seven qualities
that make a great leader.

00:00:08.983 --> 00:00:10.548
- [Robot% voice] Nine.

00:00:10.548 --> 00:00:11.874
Eight.

00:00:11.874 --> 00:00:15.056
(dubstep music)

00:00:15.056 --> 00:00:16.449
Seven.

00:00:16.449 --> 00:00:18.086
- So I often get asked by entrepreneurs,

00:00:18.086 --> 00:00:20.541
how do I be a better leader,
how do I be a better guide,

00:00:20.541 --> 00:00:22.416
a better mentor for my team?

00:00:22.416 --> 00:00:23.950
And so today, I'm going to share with you

00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:27.617
my seven ways for you
to be a better leader.

00:00:31.023 --> 00:00:33.717
Way number one is to
have more self awareness.

00:00:33.717 --> 00:00:36.235
This is super important, if
you want to be able to know

00:00:36.235 --> 00:00:39.929
how you can help other people
learn, grow, and develop,

00:00:39.929 --> 00:00:42.367
you need to first understand yourself.

00:00:42.367 --> 00:00:45.210
Before you can understand,
truly understand other people,

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:47.464
you need to first understand yourself.

00:00:47.464 --> 00:00:49.866
And so the path, the lens
through which I see the world,

00:00:49.866 --> 00:00:51.992
my path is through finding your one word.

00:00:51.992 --> 00:00:54.639
I think, in order to help other
people find their one word,

00:00:54.639 --> 00:00:56.822
you need to know yours
first, you need to understand

00:00:56.822 --> 00:00:59.202
the type of people that you
want to surround yourself with,

00:00:59.202 --> 00:01:00.305
you need to understand what kind

00:01:00.305 --> 00:01:01.848
of business that you're trying to create,

00:01:01.848 --> 00:01:03.085
you need to understand the mission,

00:01:03.085 --> 00:01:04.981
the mission that you're on, and it starts

00:01:04.981 --> 00:01:06.884
with the self-awareness of understanding

00:01:06.884 --> 00:01:09.428
your single most important core value

00:01:09.428 --> 00:01:12.642
that you want to drive
business growth through.

00:01:12.642 --> 00:01:15.453
If you can't understand yourself first,

00:01:15.453 --> 00:01:16.614
then you're going to
have a really hard time

00:01:16.614 --> 00:01:18.864
understanding other people.

00:01:21.666 --> 00:01:24.600
Number two is unlocking
their self-awareness.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:27.130
All the people on your
team, if you can help them

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:28.641
through the process of figuring out

00:01:28.641 --> 00:01:30.940
what they stand for,
what their one word is,

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:32.983
what their most important core value is,

00:01:32.983 --> 00:01:36.130
then it makes it easier for
them to understand themselves,

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:40.274
and for you to guide them
and help them on their path.

00:01:40.274 --> 00:01:42.585
If you know that somebody
really values creativity,

00:01:42.585 --> 00:01:43.444
then it's looking at,

00:01:43.444 --> 00:01:44.883
"How do I give them more creative tasks?"

00:01:44.883 --> 00:01:46.996
If somebody really values grit, then

00:01:46.996 --> 00:01:49.400
"How do I give them tasks
that they can grind out?"

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:52.558
For them to understand who they are,

00:01:52.558 --> 00:01:54.857
then they can carve out
a path to themselves,

00:01:54.857 --> 00:01:56.784
hopefully inside your organization,

00:01:56.784 --> 00:01:58.734
but it may also mean
that they have to leave

00:01:58.734 --> 00:02:00.812
because there's a mismatch of values.

00:02:00.812 --> 00:02:03.273
Their skills may be perfectly aligned

00:02:03.273 --> 00:02:05.816
with what you need, but
their values are not,

00:02:05.816 --> 00:02:08.568
and so it's not a good
long-term fit for your business.

00:02:08.568 --> 00:02:10.158
Unlocking their self-awareness, I think,

00:02:10.158 --> 00:02:12.178
is one of the greatest
gifts that you can give

00:02:12.178 --> 00:02:14.581
as a leader, because in my view,

00:02:14.581 --> 00:02:17.246
the role of a leader
is to unlock potential.

00:02:17.246 --> 00:02:19.470
It's to see people where they're at now,

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:21.734
and help them understand
where they can go,

00:02:21.734 --> 00:02:23.255
and help them understand that the ceiling

00:02:23.255 --> 00:02:25.705
that they have for
themselves are nowhere near

00:02:25.705 --> 00:02:28.063
the ceilings that they
could actually bust through.

00:02:28.063 --> 00:02:29.816
So a lot of people feel like
they could be doing more,

00:02:29.816 --> 00:02:31.604
but they don't know what
they should be doing.

00:02:31.604 --> 00:02:34.158
That's your job as a
leader, to help them see

00:02:34.158 --> 00:02:37.395
that untapped potential,
and help them realize it.

00:02:37.395 --> 00:02:39.534
And again, for me the
start is understanding

00:02:39.534 --> 00:02:43.451
the self-awareness,
figuring out your one word.

00:02:45.559 --> 00:02:47.996
Number three is put them first.

00:02:47.996 --> 00:02:49.809
This is really, really, really important,

00:02:49.809 --> 00:02:52.549
and one thing that I don't
think enough leaders do.

00:02:52.549 --> 00:02:55.648
My job, my goal, my intent,
every time I'm meeting

00:02:55.648 --> 00:02:58.470
with somebody who works for
me, is to put them first.

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:01.082
I want to help them as a human being.

00:03:01.082 --> 00:03:04.019
And ideally, it matches
up with my company,

00:03:04.019 --> 00:03:06.190
what I'm trying to do,
but again, it may not.

00:03:06.190 --> 00:03:08.350
Like, what they want to do may not be

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:10.097
the best fit for my company,

00:03:10.097 --> 00:03:12.814
and I don't want to hold
them back in a position

00:03:12.814 --> 00:03:15.328
because it's safe, or easy, I want them

00:03:15.328 --> 00:03:17.600
off doing the thing that
makes them come alive.

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:20.340
And so, this is actually a hard concept

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:22.685
for people to understand when
they first sit down with me,

00:03:22.685 --> 00:03:26.468
because it's so easy for the
leader to put themselves first,

00:03:26.468 --> 00:03:28.791
to see everything through
the lens of the business.

00:03:28.791 --> 00:03:30.812
Well, I think the great goal of a leader

00:03:30.812 --> 00:03:33.285
should be to put your people first,

00:03:33.285 --> 00:03:37.337
try to help them do what's best for them.

00:03:37.337 --> 00:03:39.728
That's what gets them to
really open up and tell you,

00:03:39.728 --> 00:03:41.574
and maybe there's a fit
within your organization,

00:03:41.574 --> 00:03:43.478
or maybe it means they need to leave.

00:03:43.478 --> 00:03:46.276
But either way, in every
conversation, I'm trying

00:03:46.276 --> 00:03:50.443
to help my person grow to
the best of their ability.

00:03:53.556 --> 00:03:55.202
Number four is make a plan.

00:03:55.202 --> 00:03:57.096
When you understay what they want to do,

00:03:57.096 --> 00:03:59.013
what their goals are,
what their ambitions are,

00:03:59.013 --> 00:04:00.197
what they want to accomplish,

00:04:00.197 --> 00:04:01.788
and you know what their one word is,

00:04:01.788 --> 00:04:03.321
then you know what their values are.

00:04:03.321 --> 00:04:05.097
Then, it's making a plan for them.

00:04:05.097 --> 00:04:07.465
Okay, what does the
world look like for you

00:04:07.465 --> 00:04:10.089
in an ideal spot, like,
where do you want to go?

00:04:10.089 --> 00:04:12.146
What are the next things
you want to accomplish

00:04:12.146 --> 00:04:14.793
in a one year, five year, 10 year window?

00:04:14.793 --> 00:04:16.095
What are some of the things that you want

00:04:16.095 --> 00:04:18.150
to get out there and do,
and how can I help you?

00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:20.356
And helping put some structure around it,

00:04:20.356 --> 00:04:23.966
so that it's not just
some idea that they have,

00:04:23.966 --> 00:04:25.789
it's helping them create it.

00:04:25.789 --> 00:04:28.004
A lot of people, when they
come into your organization,

00:04:28.004 --> 00:04:31.640
they may not have set goals
for what they want to do.

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:34.183
Right, they know what kind
of makes them feel good,

00:04:34.183 --> 00:04:36.563
they may have a vision for what
they want their life to be,

00:04:36.563 --> 00:04:38.674
but they're afraid to do
it, and they don't often

00:04:38.674 --> 00:04:40.618
have people in their
life to help push them,

00:04:40.618 --> 00:04:43.071
and guide them, and make plans with them.

00:04:43.071 --> 00:04:44.773
They may not be great at
making plans themselves,

00:04:44.773 --> 00:04:46.972
and there's nobody around
them to help them with that.

00:04:46.972 --> 00:04:49.280
And so this is your
opportunity, as a leader,

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:51.255
to help them do that, and again,

00:04:51.255 --> 00:04:53.624
you're making a plan
that's in the best interest

00:04:53.624 --> 00:04:56.210
of them, and not just you.

00:04:56.210 --> 00:04:57.490
And sometimes that may mean that you

00:04:57.490 --> 00:04:59.162
are a stepping stone for them.

00:04:59.162 --> 00:05:01.159
You know, as an example
here at Toronto Dance Salsa,

00:05:01.159 --> 00:05:03.434
one of my instructors,
Lily, she actually teaches

00:05:03.434 --> 00:05:06.058
the most classes that we have right now.

00:05:06.058 --> 00:05:08.717
She teaches the most
classes, her goal long term

00:05:08.717 --> 00:05:11.387
is she sees herself on stage,
she wants to be a speaker,

00:05:11.387 --> 00:05:14.766
she wants to be, you know,
entertaining, and delighting,

00:05:14.766 --> 00:05:18.562
and inspiring audiences,
that's what she wants to do.

00:05:18.562 --> 00:05:21.476
I can't provide that
within Toronto Dance Salsa,

00:05:21.476 --> 00:05:23.497
as a setting, I can give
her tools, and resources,

00:05:23.497 --> 00:05:24.992
and knowledge, based on what I've done,

00:05:24.992 --> 00:05:27.061
because I've gone out and done
those speaking engagements.

00:05:27.061 --> 00:05:29.499
But I can give her a platform here at TDS

00:05:29.499 --> 00:05:31.380
that becomes a stepping stone.

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:33.166
Everything that she wants to
learn, and go out on stage

00:05:33.166 --> 00:05:35.071
and do, she can start
doing inside a class.

00:05:35.071 --> 00:05:37.986
She can start being inspiring,
she can start telling stores,

00:05:37.986 --> 00:05:40.156
she can start helping
students that we have

00:05:40.156 --> 00:05:43.698
coming through, live a
better life through dance.

00:05:43.698 --> 00:05:45.904
But at some point,

00:05:45.904 --> 00:05:47.053
she's going to have to break free

00:05:47.053 --> 00:05:49.942
from what she can gain here,
to go off and do her own thing.

00:05:49.942 --> 00:05:52.301
Maybe we partner up, and
that becomes a new venture.

00:05:52.301 --> 00:05:55.237
I don't know, but TDS is
a stepping stone for her.

00:05:55.237 --> 00:05:57.753
And I get that, and I love that.

00:05:57.753 --> 00:06:00.364
Like, I don't want to
tie somebody down here,

00:06:00.364 --> 00:06:02.270
whose long term goal isn't to be here.

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:04.767
But if there's a way that our paths

00:06:04.767 --> 00:06:07.390
can be mutually beneficial
for a certain amount of time,

00:06:07.390 --> 00:06:10.387
before they veer off, I love
that, let's focus on that.

00:06:10.387 --> 00:06:12.534
And so making the plan is for them,

00:06:12.534 --> 00:06:15.193
and their long-term
success, and hopefully,

00:06:15.193 --> 00:06:16.505
some of that relates to your business.

00:06:16.505 --> 00:06:19.361
And if not, push them away,
one of my favorite things to do

00:06:19.361 --> 00:06:22.205
is pushing somebody
away from the business,

00:06:22.205 --> 00:06:24.655
if it doesn't align with
what they want to do.

00:06:24.655 --> 00:06:26.069
I don't want to hold somebody back here.

00:06:26.069 --> 00:06:28.152
I want to help them grow.

00:06:30.646 --> 00:06:33.535
Number five is give them
opportunities to stretch.

00:06:33.535 --> 00:06:36.149
So when I know what somebody wants to do,

00:06:36.149 --> 00:06:38.468
and I know what their one word value is,

00:06:38.468 --> 00:06:40.375
then I want to find opportunities for them

00:06:40.375 --> 00:06:41.966
to be able to stretch, and to grow,

00:06:41.966 --> 00:06:43.394
and to build on that base.

00:06:43.394 --> 00:06:44.729
So as an example, I look at Alex,

00:06:44.729 --> 00:06:46.563
who's with me here at Toronto Dance Salsa.

00:06:46.563 --> 00:06:49.152
His first teaching opportunity came

00:06:49.152 --> 00:06:50.917
when he was dancing with Sharon,

00:06:50.917 --> 00:06:53.133
he was the assistant, he
was not the instructor.

00:06:53.133 --> 00:06:55.826
He was just adding a little
bit of advice and feedback

00:06:55.826 --> 00:06:58.231
in a class, but Sharon
was the main teacher.

00:06:58.231 --> 00:06:59.659
Sharon had an injury,

00:06:59.659 --> 00:07:01.862
and she couldn't teach the class anymore.

00:07:01.862 --> 00:07:04.244
And she asked me to come
in and teach the class.

00:07:04.244 --> 00:07:06.647
The challenge is, I can't dance with Alex,

00:07:06.647 --> 00:07:07.818
we don't have two male instructors

00:07:07.818 --> 00:07:09.735
dancing together at
the front of the class.

00:07:09.735 --> 00:07:11.324
So if I was going to teach the class,

00:07:11.324 --> 00:07:13.125
I would need to have a female assistant,

00:07:13.125 --> 00:07:15.935
and then Alex would be
left on the sidelines,

00:07:15.935 --> 00:07:18.106
and wouldn't be able to
teach, and continue to grow.

00:07:18.106 --> 00:07:19.952
So I like pushing people, I like

00:07:19.952 --> 00:07:22.553
giving people opportunities, often with

00:07:22.553 --> 00:07:24.770
very little preparation,
to see if they're going

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:26.070
to jump at it or not.

00:07:26.070 --> 00:07:28.589
So we're driving on the way to our class,

00:07:28.589 --> 00:07:32.210
and I told Alex in the car,
"Alex, we got two options here.

00:07:32.210 --> 00:07:34.708
"I can either guide the
class, I will teach it,

00:07:34.708 --> 00:07:37.831
"I will step in and run the entire night,

00:07:37.831 --> 00:07:39.482
"and I'll have a female assistant.

00:07:39.482 --> 00:07:41.664
"And you can be on the sidelines,

00:07:41.664 --> 00:07:44.033
"and help out every now and then.

00:07:44.033 --> 00:07:47.237
"Or, you could jump in
and be the instructor."

00:07:47.237 --> 00:07:48.617
It's a role that he wanted to have,

00:07:48.617 --> 00:07:51.730
but he wasn't ready for, it was a stretch.

00:07:51.730 --> 00:07:53.842
"And I'll sit on the
sidelines, and I'll guide you.

00:07:53.842 --> 00:07:55.747
"In between sets, on every song,

00:07:55.747 --> 00:07:56.769
"I'll come, I'll give you feedback,

00:07:56.769 --> 00:07:58.069
"I'll mentor you, help you get better.

00:07:58.069 --> 00:07:58.902
"You're going to make mistakes,

00:07:58.902 --> 00:08:00.345
"but we'll build you up, and grow,

00:08:00.345 --> 00:08:02.353
"and see, what do you want to
do, what would you rather do?"

00:08:02.353 --> 00:08:05.279
And he took the shot to
become the instructor.

00:08:05.279 --> 00:08:07.566
And then my role, for
the rest of the semester,

00:08:07.566 --> 00:08:10.194
was guiding him, mentoring
him, and training him.

00:08:10.194 --> 00:08:13.393
And so I love giving stretch
opportunities for people,

00:08:13.393 --> 00:08:16.864
pushing them beyond what
their comfort zone is.

00:08:16.864 --> 00:08:20.347
And my believing in them, as a leader,

00:08:20.347 --> 00:08:22.321
helps them believe in themselves.

00:08:22.321 --> 00:08:24.364
They may not believe in
themselves and ability to do it,

00:08:24.364 --> 00:08:26.663
but me saying, "I think you can do it,

00:08:26.663 --> 00:08:29.014
"and I'm here to help
you," helps push them

00:08:29.014 --> 00:08:32.383
through the discomfort to
create something amazing.

00:08:32.383 --> 00:08:34.902
And I think that is one of
the primary responsibilities,

00:08:34.902 --> 00:08:36.865
as a leader, is to help them push

00:08:36.865 --> 00:08:38.812
through their discomfort
to reach a ceiling

00:08:38.812 --> 00:08:42.979
that they didn't even know
existed, and bust through it.

00:08:44.825 --> 00:08:46.754
Number six is hold them accountable.

00:08:46.754 --> 00:08:49.725
This, as well, is a super
important one, because people

00:08:49.725 --> 00:08:52.175
tend to not hold themselves
accountable enough.

00:08:52.175 --> 00:08:53.871
People have goals, people make plans,

00:08:53.871 --> 00:08:56.518
people have aspirations, people
set New Year's resolutions,

00:08:56.518 --> 00:08:57.713
and then they don't follow through.

00:08:57.713 --> 00:08:59.118
Most people don't follow
through on the things

00:08:59.118 --> 00:09:01.742
they say they want to
do, is important to them.

00:09:01.742 --> 00:09:04.958
If you make a plan, if
you understand the person,

00:09:04.958 --> 00:09:07.346
you understand their one
word, and you understand

00:09:07.346 --> 00:09:09.844
the credo, the three things
that make up their one word,

00:09:09.844 --> 00:09:13.501
now you have the ability
to hold them accountable.

00:09:13.501 --> 00:09:16.205
If you look at why they're
not achieving their plan,

00:09:16.205 --> 00:09:18.165
why they haven't made the steps forward,

00:09:18.165 --> 00:09:19.675
you can look at what they stand for.

00:09:19.675 --> 00:09:21.476
What is your one word,
what are the three things

00:09:21.476 --> 00:09:23.253
that break it down, if, for example,

00:09:23.253 --> 00:09:25.637
one of them is creativity.

00:09:25.637 --> 00:09:26.490
Okay?

00:09:26.490 --> 00:09:30.565
Are you being creative, in
your accomplishing this goal?

00:09:30.565 --> 00:09:32.576
Are you being creative
enough in your work?

00:09:32.576 --> 00:09:35.792
If one of them is grit, you
can look at that and say,

00:09:35.792 --> 00:09:38.346
"Okay, like, why aren't
you following through?

00:09:38.346 --> 00:09:40.041
"Grit is your, is part of your credo,

00:09:40.041 --> 00:09:41.856
"it's part of your one word.

00:09:41.856 --> 00:09:44.335
"You need to stop letting
yourself down, like,

00:09:44.335 --> 00:09:45.438
"you saw that you're letting me down.

00:09:45.438 --> 00:09:47.144
"You're letting yourself
down, you say these things

00:09:47.144 --> 00:09:49.966
"are important to you, and
you're not living up to them.

00:09:49.966 --> 00:09:52.067
It's shining a mirror of truth.

00:09:52.067 --> 00:09:54.006
Here's the problem I
think most leaders make.

00:09:54.006 --> 00:09:56.923
Most leaders assign their own life,

00:09:57.953 --> 00:10:01.552
their own values, onto somebody else.

00:10:01.552 --> 00:10:05.546
If I really value
creativity, and you don't,

00:10:05.546 --> 00:10:09.658
then me telling you to be
creative is not going to help you.

00:10:09.658 --> 00:10:11.987
You're going to feel like
you're doing it for me.

00:10:11.987 --> 00:10:13.950
You're going to feel like
you never really fit in.

00:10:13.950 --> 00:10:15.285
And I don't want you
to do anything for me.

00:10:15.285 --> 00:10:18.257
I want you to do it for you,
and it helps both of us.

00:10:18.257 --> 00:10:20.834
You have to ultimately
want to do it for you.

00:10:20.834 --> 00:10:24.503
I'm creating a plan to help
you help you, that's it.

00:10:24.503 --> 00:10:26.558
And along the way, that helps me, as well.

00:10:26.558 --> 00:10:29.124
Mutually beneficial,
and so you have to know

00:10:29.124 --> 00:10:31.335
what to push people on.

00:10:31.335 --> 00:10:34.195
You have to know where they
are letting themselves down,

00:10:34.195 --> 00:10:37.248
by figuring out their
one word and their credo,

00:10:37.248 --> 00:10:39.305
so that you have the
mirror to shine up, and say

00:10:39.305 --> 00:10:41.186
"This is what you say you stand for.

00:10:41.186 --> 00:10:44.193
"You're letting yourself
down here, stop it."

00:10:44.193 --> 00:10:46.062
And they have nothing to
come back to you with,

00:10:46.062 --> 00:10:49.174
because it's the truth,
because they realize that

00:10:49.174 --> 00:10:52.874
they haven't done what they
want to do for themselves.

00:10:52.874 --> 00:10:55.315
This is all for them, and
nobody else in their life

00:10:55.315 --> 00:10:56.790
is holding them accountable.

00:10:56.790 --> 00:10:59.123
That's your job as a leader.

00:11:01.710 --> 00:11:04.708
And number seven is
inspire with your actions.

00:11:04.708 --> 00:11:06.528
So leaders can come up
and give great speeches,

00:11:06.528 --> 00:11:09.026
and tell people what to do, and tell them

00:11:09.026 --> 00:11:11.523
why they started this business,
and all of that great stuff.

00:11:11.523 --> 00:11:13.706
And it's definitely needed,
sharing the mission,

00:11:13.706 --> 00:11:15.807
sharing the vision,
sharing why it's important,

00:11:15.807 --> 00:11:17.130
sharing the history and the story,

00:11:17.130 --> 00:11:19.313
and your connection to
it is super important.

00:11:19.313 --> 00:11:21.484
But it's also not enough just to talk.

00:11:21.484 --> 00:11:23.922
And if you're not great at
talking, then it doesn't have

00:11:23.922 --> 00:11:26.999
to be your only lever that you can pull.

00:11:26.999 --> 00:11:29.156
You can inspire with your actions.

00:11:29.156 --> 00:11:31.886
I could tell somebody
"You need to work hard.

00:11:31.886 --> 00:11:33.477
"Look, you said working hard is part

00:11:33.477 --> 00:11:36.949
"of your one word and credo,
you need to work harder.

00:11:36.949 --> 00:11:39.131
"You're not working hard enough."

00:11:39.131 --> 00:11:42.670
And I can push them there,
but I can also model it.

00:11:42.670 --> 00:11:44.911
They can come in and see me on a Wednesday

00:11:44.911 --> 00:11:47.374
shooting 70 videos for the month,

00:11:47.374 --> 00:11:49.406
and see how I'm working hard.

00:11:49.406 --> 00:11:52.073
Your actions can inspire action.

00:11:53.085 --> 00:11:55.202
You don't need to be the
world's most charismatic,

00:11:55.202 --> 00:11:57.553
most, you know, greatest speech writer

00:11:57.553 --> 00:12:00.435
and delivery person, you don't
have to be Martin Luther King

00:12:00.435 --> 00:12:04.292
at the front of your
organization, rallying the troops.

00:12:04.292 --> 00:12:06.731
If that is you, great,
lean in on it heavily.

00:12:06.731 --> 00:12:08.577
But if it's not, you don't
have to be like that.

00:12:08.577 --> 00:12:11.280
You can inspire people through
the actions that you take.

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:14.138
People should look at you,
and want to be like you.

00:12:14.138 --> 00:12:17.447
They want to emulate some of
those characteristics of you.

00:12:17.447 --> 00:12:19.255
That's why I started the Mentor Me series,

00:12:19.255 --> 00:12:21.505
to try to learn from these
super successful people,

00:12:21.505 --> 00:12:23.526
spend more time with them,

00:12:23.526 --> 00:12:25.917
so that I could be the best version of me.

00:12:25.917 --> 00:12:29.577
When I asked the people on my
team at the end of the year,

00:12:29.577 --> 00:12:31.222
"What do you want from me this year?

00:12:31.222 --> 00:12:34.588
"How can I be a better role
model, guide, mentor for you?"

00:12:34.588 --> 00:12:36.467
The number one answer that
came back consistently

00:12:36.467 --> 00:12:39.174
was just more time, they
wanted more time with me.

00:12:39.174 --> 00:12:41.787
And so I had to adjust my
schedule to give them more time.

00:12:41.787 --> 00:12:44.527
They want more time because the actions

00:12:44.527 --> 00:12:47.747
are leading to inspired
action on their part.

00:12:47.747 --> 00:12:49.261
So those are my seven qualities

00:12:49.261 --> 00:12:50.991
of what I think makes up a great leader.

00:12:50.991 --> 00:12:53.127
I'd love to know, what do you guys think?

00:12:53.127 --> 00:12:55.390
Which quality did you agree the most with?

00:12:55.390 --> 00:12:56.597
Which quality you feel like you need

00:12:56.597 --> 00:12:58.037
to work a little bit more on?

00:12:58.037 --> 00:12:59.314
And did I miss an eight, nine, 10

00:12:59.314 --> 00:13:00.568
that you want to add to the list?

00:13:00.568 --> 00:13:02.562
Leave it down in the
comments section below,

00:13:02.562 --> 00:13:04.155
and we'll join in the discussion.

00:13:04.155 --> 00:13:05.792
Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:13:05.792 --> 00:13:07.778
I believe in you, I hope you continue

00:13:07.778 --> 00:13:11.041
to believe in yourself, and
whatever your one word is.

00:13:11.041 --> 00:13:13.547
Much love, I'll see you soon.

00:13:13.547 --> 00:13:16.092
(whooshing)
(bomb blast)

